# Expectation record for 02_constant_expressions/004_sizeof_alignof.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_sizeof_alignof
area               = 02_constant_expressions
description        = sizeof and _Alignof as constant expressions over scalars, pointers, arrays and aggregates, width-normalized around one exact target-keyed pointer-width anchor so that every cross-target-variable quantity is printed as a relation rather than as a number and all four backends stay comparable, with folded and volatile-runtime twins
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined behaviour and depends on no unspecified behaviour, which is the
precondition both differential oracles rest on rather than a stylistic preference: undefined
behaviour would license either compiler to do anything at all, so a divergence between them would
prove nothing about either. The paragraphs below are the written argument required of every program
in this suite; the warning gate and the sanitizer gate are its machine half.
SIZEOF DOES NOT EVALUATE ITS OPERAND (C11 6.5.3.4p2), AND THAT IS PROVED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. A
function is named inside a sizeof operand and a volatile counter records whether it was called; the
counter is read before and after, and the printed lines show that sizeof(probe()) left probe_calls at
zero and that calling probe for real afterwards made the counter exactly one. Because the counter is
volatile the compiler must store to it and must re-read it, so "the call did not happen" is a fact
about the generated program rather than about the optimizer's mood. Because the operand is
unevaluated, the volatile objects and the function call that appear inside a sizeof are never
accessed there, so no side effect is hidden inside an operand that is not evaluated, and naming the
inactive member of a union inside sizeof is safe for the same reason: the operand is not evaluated,
so no inactive member is ever read.
POINTER ARITHMETIC STAYS IN RANGE. arr is walked by a pointer from arr to arr + ARR_ELEMS with the
loop test walk != stop, so the body runs for indices 0 through 6 of a seven-element array; the
one-past-the-end pointer stop is FORMED, COMPARED and SUBTRACTED -- which C11 6.5.6p8 permits
explicitly -- but is never dereferenced. mat is walked over exactly its 3 rows and 5 columns, driven
by volatile copies of those two extents. The two one-element-per-target arrays declared with a sizeof
and an _Alignof bound are written only at index sizeof arr - 1, which is in bounds by construction,
and read only at index 0 and that same last index. Every pointer difference is taken between two
elements of one and the same array object, which is the only case C11 6.5.6p9 defines: the difference
&probe_pair[1] - &probe_pair[0] is 1, printed as an int after a cast, so nothing depends on the width
of ptrdiff_t, and no address is printed anywhere.
NO STORAGE IS READ UNINITIALIZED. The arrays whose bounds come from sizeof and _Alignof have static
storage duration and are therefore zero-initialized, so reading their untouched first element is
defined and prints 0, and their last elements are assigned before they are read. probe_calls is a
volatile static and therefore starts at a definite zero. Every automatic object is initialized in its
own declaration or assigned before its first read, and every aggregate that is read back is
initialized in its own declaration.
NO ARITHMETIC CAN OVERFLOW OR DIVIDE BY ZERO. Every value is a small byte count, an element count, a
0/1 relation or a small sum: the largest printed value is 70000, a short-to-int conversion of a value
stored in an int member, and the largest computed values are mat_sum at 120 and walk_sum at 58. All
size arithmetic is performed in the unsigned type sizeof yields, where C11 6.2.5p9 defines every
result modularly, and each such value is narrowed to int by an explicit cast only after it is known
to be small -- 4, 8, 12, 28, 30 and similar -- so the gate's -Wconversion has nothing to object to.
In the folded variant each divisor is a sizeof of a complete object type or an _Alignof, both of
which the standard guarantees are non-zero; in the runtime twin each divisor is a volatile object
initialized from exactly one of those same quantities in its own declaration and never assigned
afterwards, so it cannot be zero either, and every one of those operands is unsigned, so no division
is INT_MIN / -1 and no remainder depends on the sign of its operands. There is no shift anywhere in
the program.
THE FLOATING COMPARISONS ARE EXACT BY CONSTRUCTION. 1.5f and 0.5f are exactly representable in binary
floating point, so inv_obj.f == 1.5f compares two identical values rather than posing a tolerance
question, and no floating-point value is printed at all.
TWENTY-ONE _Static_assert DECLARATIONS PIN THE INVARIANTS THIS PROGRAM DEPENDS ON BEFORE IT RUNS --
the fixed widths of char, short, int, long long, float and double, that long and every pointer
flavour share one width, that size_t and ptrdiff_t are that width, the string-literal and
character-constant sizes, the long double ordering laws and the aggregate layout laws -- so a
mis-folded sizeof is a translation failure rather than a silently wrong number. Every one of them is
SATISFIED, which is a soundness requirement rather than a preference: both mandated oracles compare
the observable behaviour of a binary that built, so a deliberately failing assertion would stop both
compilers and leave nothing to compare.
EVERY CHARACTER READ BACK IS CONVERTED THROUGH unsigned char BEFORE IT IS PRINTED, so no plain-char
signedness difference can reach stdout, and no member offset is ever assumed: a member is read by
name, never through a computed offset, so no padding byte is observed. There is no aliasing violation
-- the union is read only through the member last written, and every object is accessed through its
own declared type -- and nothing depends on the relative addresses of unrelated objects.
SEQUENCING IS EXPLICIT. Each expression reads each volatile object at most once, so no object is
modified twice between sequence points, the volatile access sequence is fixed, and each volatile
increment inside probe stands alone in its own statement. No printf call receives more than one
argument whose evaluation has an effect: every argument is a sizeof, an _Alignof, a comparison of
those, or a snapshot taken into a plain local beforehand, so nothing depends on the unspecified order
in which a call's arguments are evaluated.
NO HEADER IS NAMED ANYWHERE and the single libc prototype the program needs, printf, is declared by
hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`. bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a
bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so a directive
naming that header would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a) while succeeding on the reference side --
a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler under test. _Alignof is a C11
keyword and needs no header, and stddef.h is deliberately not named. Nothing in the file is a GCC
extension, so no include exception and no gate deviation is available to area 02 and none is needed;
the corpus has exactly two header exceptions, area 07's stdarg.h and the dedicated bundled-header
probe in area 12.
size_t, ptrdiff_t and intptr_t therefore cannot be NAMED in this program at all, and their widths are
probed without naming them: sizeof yields a size_t, so sizeof(sizeof(int)) is size_t's own width, and
a pointer difference yields a ptrdiff_t, so sizeof of one is ptrdiff_t's width. That is an advantage
rather than a workaround -- it measures the widths the compiler actually implements rather than the
widths a header declares.
THIS RECORD DECLARES NO WARNING-GATE DEVIATION: no ub_audit_flags key appears above, so the program
is audited under the suite's full default gate. It is clean under -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers,
and it builds and runs clean under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all run natively to completion. Both gates are reference-compiler-only and are
never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing. All four targets, all three optimization levels and all three
oracles are enabled, and no expected-divergence marker is attached. These notes exist to
record how that was achieved, because a program in this area could easily have had to narrow:
constant evaluation reaches straight into the type system, which is where the four supported
targets differ.
That is a strong claim for THIS program in particular, because sizeof and _Alignof are the two
places in the language where a target's ABI becomes an observable integer, so these notes record
the normalization in detail. The quantities that differ, as MEASURED with the four reference
drivers oracle (a) uses, are: sizeof(long), sizeof(void *), the width of size_t and the width of
ptrdiff_t, all 8 bytes on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 and 4 bytes on i686; sizeof(long double),
16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes respectively; _Alignof(long), _Alignof(long long) and _Alignof(double), 8
on the three 64-bit targets and 4 on i686; and _Alignof(long double), 16, 4, 16 and 16. The
repository's own target table fixes pointer and long at 8 bytes with ELF64 on x86-64, AArch64 and
RISC-V 64 and at 4 bytes with ELF32 on i686 (docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines
457-462). Note that _Alignof(double) is 4 on i686 because _Alignof reports the ABI alignment the
i386 System V ABI requires rather than the preferred alignment; the two must not be conflated.
WHAT THIS PROGRAM DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. The easy answer would be to restrict the target list
to the three 64-bit targets and print the raw numbers. That is forbidden: constraint C3 says no
feature is excluded because it is difficult, and dropping i686 would remove three of this
program's twelve cells -- and with them the only ILP32 backend, which is the only target on
which any of the widths tabulated above actually differs, so it is exactly where a width defect
could hide with nothing left to notice it. All four targets are kept and the program normalizes
instead.
THE NORMALIZATION RULEBOOK, in the order it is applied. (1) A quantity measured identical on all
four targets is printed RAW, as a value rather than as a predicate, so a target that disagreed
shows up as a divergence on that one line instead of being quietly accepted by a predicate that
held for the wrong reason: sizeof char, short, int, long long, float and double are 1, 2, 4, 8, 4
and 8, _Alignof char, short, int and float are 1, 2, 4 and 4, sizeof and _Alignof of _Bool and of
the enumerated type are 1 and 4 with alignment 1 and 4, sizeof(struct invariant) is 12 with
alignment 4, sizeof(union invariant_union) is 8 with alignment 4, sizeof arr is 28, sizeof mat is
30, and the sizeof-derived array bounds and enumerator are all 4. (2) A quantity that differs by
target is printed only as a RELATION between two such quantities, which yields the same 0 or 1
everywhere -- sizeof(long), sizeof(void *), sizeof(char *), sizeof(int *),
sizeof(void (*)(void)), sizeof(sizeof(int)) and the pointer-difference width all appear inside
comparisons that read 1 on every target, and the width-bearing aggregate is never printed as a
size but only as sizeof(struct widthful) == 3 * sizeof(void *), which holds at both widths. (3)
Relations alone are NOT sufficient, and this is the trap worth stating plainly: a relation is
satisfied by two values that are wrong in the same direction, so sizeof(long) == sizeof(void *)
prints 1 even if both were 2. The whole pointer-width family is therefore anchored to ONE exact,
target-keyed equality -- ptr_width_exact -- and every other member is tied to that anchor by a
relation, so a single number per target pins the entire family exactly: long, char *, int *, a
function pointer, a pointer to array, a pointer to pointer, size_t, ptrdiff_t and the alignment
of all of them. (4) A permissive bound is never used where an exact answer exists: a range such as
4 <= x <= 8 accepts 5, 6 and 7 and accepts the wrong choice between 4 and 8, so a real
code-generation defect would still print 1 and both the reference oracle and the golden record
would agree with it.
(5) long double IS THE ONE TYPE GIVEN BOUNDS RATHER THAN AN EXACT PIN, because no exact answer is
available to assert. Its representation is implementation-defined -- 16 bytes on x86-64 and 12 on
i686 for the same x87 80-bit format differently padded, 16 on AArch64 and RISC-V 64 for IEEE
binary128 -- and C11 permits an implementation to give long double exactly double's
representation. Its raw size is therefore never printed. What is printed instead are the three
facts that hold everywhere: it is at least as wide as double, at least as strictly aligned as
double, and its size is a whole multiple of its alignment. The type is therefore exercised here
rather than dropped for being awkward. Cross-backend VALUE equality for long double is the
subject of 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.c, where it is a RECORDED
EXCLUSION -- oracle_b disabled, with the measured representations as its basis -- and deliberately
NOT a marker: the marker contract admits only an observed divergence backed by captured evidence,
and an implementation-defined representation difference is not a bcc defect to begin with. Here
nothing is excluded at all, because nothing representation-dependent is printed.
THE TARGET-KEYED ANCHOR IS KEYED TO THE ARCHITECTURE, NOT TO A WIDTH MACRO, and the difference
matters. The architecture is a categorical fact the harness fixes when it selects the cell's
target, whereas a width macro such as __SIZEOF_POINTER__ is a numeric claim by the compiler
under test, and checking that claim against itself would be circular. The #else arm is a HARD
STOP -- an #error naming the four architecture macros it looked for -- rather than a fallback: a
compiler that predefines none of them cannot be given an exact expectation, and quietly falling
back to a permissive one would turn rule 4 into a comment, so a fifth target cannot silently be
judged against another target's width. A refusal to compile is reported with a named message and
is reproducible from this record's own command template; a silently weakened test is not reported
at all.
TWO AGGREGATE SIZES ARE PRINTED RAW, and their shapes were chosen so that they could be.
struct invariant is char, short, int, float and sizes at 12 bytes with alignment 4 on all four
targets; the union sizes at 8 with alignment 4. Both were measured on every target before the
raw values were written here. An aggregate whose padding depended on the alignment of double or
long long would have sized differently on i686, where those alignments are 4 rather than 8, and
is deliberately absent.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION IN THE OUTPUT ITSELF. Every printed value is an int printed with %d, so each
sizeof and _Alignof result is cast to int before it reaches the variadic call and no size_t is
ever passed; where the program computes a value too wide for int, that value is written in long
long with LL-suffixed literals and printed with %lld, which is 64 bits on every enabled target.
No plain long, no size_t, no ptrdiff_t, no floating-point value and no pointer value is printed
anywhere.
PLAIN-char SIGNEDNESS. Plain char was MEASURED as signed on x86-64 and i686 and unsigned on
AArch64 and RISC-V 64. Every character read-back is cast through unsigned char first, so no
plain-char value is printed as a signed value and no printed line depends on that choice.
THE EXECUTION CHARACTER SET IS A SEPARATE IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED PROPERTY FROM CHAR SIGNEDNESS,
AND IT IS PINNED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Four printed lines carry a character's numeric code:
struct_invariant_readback prints 120 for 'x', structp_readback prints 121 for 'y',
nested_readback prints 84 for 'T', and ptrtab_first_chars prints 97, 98, 99 and 100 for the
initials of the four string-table entries. Those codes exist because a read-back has to print
something, and a character member is the narrowest way to prove that the byte an initializer
placed at a computed offset is the byte that comes back out. The (int)(unsigned char) conversion
applied to each removes plain char's SIGNEDNESS and does nothing whatever about the character
set: C11 5.2.1 leaves the members of the execution character set and their codes
implementation-defined, so 'x' is 120 on an ASCII-family implementation and could be something
else on one that is not.
The source therefore pins every one of them, and the two letters that appear only in
initializers as well, with _Static_assert declarations for 'x' == 120, 'y' == 121, 'z' == 122,
'w' == 119, 'T' == 84 and 'a' == 97 && 'b' == 98 && 'c' == 99 && 'd' == 100. All four enabled
targets were measured to use the same ASCII-family basic execution character set, so every
condition holds on every cell of this matrix. The assertions are what keep the oracles honest:
on an implementation whose character set differed, the cell would FAIL TO TRANSLATE naming the
reason -- accusing the environment, which is where the difference actually lives -- instead of
printing different numbers that oracle (a) or oracle (b) would charge to the compiler under
test. A future target that disagreed would announce itself at the build, and the correct
response would be to narrow this record's target list with the reason recorded here, never to
delete the assertions or the read-backs they protect.
WHAT WOULD LEGITIMATELY BREAK THIS, AND HOW IT MUST THEN BE HANDLED. A conforming target on which
long were not pointer-width, or on which size_t or ptrdiff_t differed from pointer width, or on
which int were not 4 bytes, would turn one of the relation lines from 1 into 0, or change a raw
value, without being defective. That would be an implementation-defined divergence rather than a
compiler defect, and it must then be recorded as an expected divergence with this record's target
list narrowed and the reason stated in this field -- never left to surface as a finding against a
backend that did nothing wrong, and never resolved by deleting the relation, which is precisely
the coverage requirement 2 asks this program to provide.
THE EXACT PER-TARGET WIDTHS AND ALIGNMENTS THIS PROGRAM DECLINES TO PIN ARE NOT LEFT UNTESTED.
10_declarations_and_types/007_alignof_alignas.c pins them exactly, selecting the expectation from
the architecture predefined macro. The division of labour is deliberate, so that neither program
needs a restricted target list.
HERMETIC AND DETERMINISTIC. Every input is a literal in the source: nothing is read from the
clock, the environment, the command line, a file or a socket, and nothing is written anywhere.
Output is one line per semantic property claimed, in a fixed order, so a single divergent line
names the construct that produced it. No address or pointer value is printed, there is no
randomness, no locale-dependent formatting and no data-dependent iteration order, and the
program returns 0 -- well inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is
truncated by the operating system.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at
-O0, -O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 63 lines
byte-identically with exit status 0, which is why cross-backend comparison is sound, oracle (b)
is enabled with no narrowing, and oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
